import Native

{-
   Sorting a container - Analysis-Library.md §3.5 and Part 6 item 4.

   Four names over one algorithm written twice. `sort` and `sortBy` reorder a container in place;
   `sorted` and `sortedBy` leave it alone and answer a new `Array(a)`. The `By` half takes the
   comparison as a closure and the other half takes it from `Ord(a)`, which is the only difference
   between them and the reason there are four rather than two.

   **Not stable.** Two elements that compare equal may come out in either order, and no caller may
   depend on which - the partition below exchanges across the range, so equal keys are separated by
   design and restoring their order would cost the O(n) scratch this has none of. What a caller that
   needs stability writes is a comparison that breaks its own ties, which is what a stable sort would
   have been doing for it anyway and is cheaper here than everywhere.
-}

{-
   Why the four take the container and not a `[a]` - and it is the closure that decides it.

   The obvious signature is `sort(&xs: [a])`, the way `secureRandom` and `File.write` take theirs:
   the conversion at the call already turns an owner into the window, so one declaration serves the
   array, the slice, and any container of the program's own with a `Contiguous` instance. It works
   for `sort` and it cannot work for `sortBy`, for a reason that is entirely about the *caller*:

     - The conversion builds the descriptor in the caller's frame, and Local::viewOf records it as a
       view of the caller's own array.
     - A call through a function value is an `InstCallDyn`, and its arguments are assumed retained -
       a function type says nothing about whether the callee keeps what it was handed, so escape
       analysis cannot say it does not. See the note in resolve/analyze.cpp.
     - So `sortBy` retained its window, the caller's descriptor escaped, and checkEscapingViews
       reported "this borrow of xs outlives the frame that owns it" at *every* call site that owned
       the container it was sorting. Which is every real one.

   **That half has since stopped being true**, and the signature is still right. `InstCallDyn` reads
   the function type's contract now, so a callback's operands are call-bounded and `sortBy(&xs: [a],
   before)` compiles and runs - it was written and measured before this paragraph was rewritten. The
   reason it went back is a different one the note above never gave: **a container of the program's
   own reaches a `[a]` parameter only if the conversion knows how to build the window**, and
   `convertToMutableArgument` knows the two owners the language has - `Array(a)` and `[a *n]` - not a
   user type with a `Contiguous` instance. So `Contiguous(c, a)` does *not* accept exactly what `[a]`
   accepts; it accepts strictly more, and the difference is every container outside this library.

   Taking `&xs: c` also keeps the descriptor on this side of the call, which is what mattered when
   the paragraph above did.

   The two `Ord` halves would compile either way and are written the same way regardless, because
   four functions that do the same thing should not have two signatures.
-}

{-
   The algorithm, and why it is here twice.

   An introsort: median-of-three quicksort, insertion sort under a small range, and heapsort when the
   recursion goes deeper than a good pivot ever needs. That last part is what makes the O(n log n)
   worst case true rather than typical - median-of-three has adversarial inputs, and a standard
   library's sort is the one function most likely to be handed data someone else chose.

   **Every element movement is a `swap` of two subscripts.** No pivot is copied, no element is
   lifted into a temporary, and nothing is written that would need `TrivialCopy(a)`: the pivot is
   *parked* at
   `low` and compared against in place, which is the whole reason the partition below is Hoare's
   rather than Lomuto's. So `sort` works on an `Array(String)` and on any other element type that
   owns something, which is the case a sort exists for.

   **Written twice rather than once over a comparison class**, and the reason has changed since this
   was decided. The class is

       class Precedes(t, a):
           fn precedes(self: t, left: a, right: a) -> Bool

   with an instance for `Ord` and one holding a closure, which is one algorithm and static dispatch
   at both. It used not to compile - "this implements a class function, so it cannot keep left
   beyond the call", because a generic body cannot see which implementation runs, takes the
   declaration's word that a borrowed argument's extent is the call, and calling a *function value*
   with one was exactly what that word forbade. **That is no longer true.** `InstCallDyn` reads the
   function type's contract now (Analysis-Borrows.md §8.4), the closure instance compiles, and the
   whole engine was rewritten over the class and put back.

   What it is blocked on now is one line: `ByFunction {before: before}`. The order has to be a
   *value* the engine is handed, so the closure instance has to hold the caller's comparator, and
   there are three ways to hold one and none of them is available:

   - **`->before`**, which compiles and consumes it. That is a change to the public API for the sake
     of an internal refactor - the caller loses a comparator it may want to sort twice with - and it
     cascades, since `sortedBy` would have to consume its own to pass it on.
   - **`copy(before)`**: there is no `Copy` instance for a function value, and there should not be a
     naive one - copying a capturing closure copies an environment somebody owns.
   - **a borrowed field**, `data ByFunction(a) {before: cmp'((a, a) -> Bool)}`, which is the right
     answer and is §4.7's stored loan exactly. Two things stop it, and both are about *function*
     values rather than about loans: a group cannot be bound at an expression site, so there is no
     way to write the constructor, and a `'((a, a) -> Bool)` is not callable - "this expression is
     not callable". Analysis-Borrows.md §5.3 is where the second one lives.

   So the duplication stays, and what it is waiting for is a loan slot that reaches a closure rather
   than anything about comparison. Five small bodies twice is what that is worth meanwhile;
   `Reduce.yana` beside this makes the same trade six times over.
-}

{-
   What these now accept, and used not to: a fixed array.

   `[a *n]` had no `Contiguous` instance, and the reason was not about sorting - a parametric
   instance of an `iter fn` could not have its continuation result inferred with specialization
   declined, so the instance compiled in one build and not the other. Everywhere else that cost
   nothing, because a fixed array reaches a `[a]` parameter through the conversion at the call; here
   there was no `[a]` parameter to reach, for the reason at the top of this file, so `sort(fixed)`
   was the one place it showed - and it was rejected badly, with "internal: a generic call was
   deferred outside a generic function" rather than a sentence about a missing instance.

   The instance is there now and `sort(fixed)` compiles - see the note beside `Length([a *n])` in
   `Container.yana`, and `test/resolve/FixedContainer.yana`, which sorts one in both builds.
-}

-- Under this many elements a range is insertion-sorted rather than partitioned. Twelve is where
-- `test/bench/programs/Sort.yana` put it and there is no reason for this to disagree with the
-- program that measures it.
let smallRange = 12 :: Size

{-
   Insertion sort, which is the whole of the answer for a short range and the tail of it for a long
   one.

   Swapping down rather than opening a hole and closing it. The hole form is one move per shifted
   element against a swap's three, and it needs the moved element to live somewhere while the shift
   runs - which is a duplicate for an `Int` and an owned value with no place to be for a `String`.
   A `swap` of two subscripts is what both element types can do, so it is what this does.
-}
fn (Ord(a)) insertionRange(&xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = low + 1

    while i <= high:
        let &j = i
        while j > low && xs[j] < xs[j - 1]:
            swap(xs[j], xs[j - 1])
            j = j - 1

        i = i + 1

{-
   Hoare's partition, with the median of three parked at `low`.

   The pivot stays where it is and every comparison reads it out of the container, which is what
   makes this work for an element that cannot be copied - and it costs nothing, because `xs[low]` is
   a load the same address every time and is exactly what a register would hold.

   Median of three and not the first element: a range that is already sorted is the input a
   first-element pivot turns into O(n²), and it is the input a program most often has. The three
   comparisons that order low, mid and high are also the three that leave `high` holding something
   not below the pivot, which is what lets the scan below stop at `high` rather than testing an index
   against the end at every step.

   **Both inner scans are guarded against running off the range, and the guards are not redundant.**
   `xs[low]` is the pivot, so `xs[low] < xs[low]` is false and the descending scan stops at `low` on
   its own - for a comparison that is a strict weak order. A caller's `before` need not be one: an
   inconsistent comparison is a bug in the caller, and what it should produce is a wrongly ordered
   container rather than a walk off the front of the array. So the guards stay, and they cost one
   already-loaded comparison per step of a loop that is doing a call's worth of work anyway.
-}
fn (Ord(a)) partitionRange(&xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size) -> Size:
    let mid = low + (high - low) / 2
    if xs[mid] < xs[low] then swap(xs[mid], xs[low])
    if xs[high] < xs[low] then swap(xs[high], xs[low])
    if xs[high] < xs[mid] then swap(xs[high], xs[mid])
    swap(xs[mid], xs[low])

    let &i = low
    let &j = high + 1
    let &crossed = False

    while !crossed:
        i = i + 1
        while i < high && xs[i] < xs[low]:
            i = i + 1

        j = j - 1
        while j > low && xs[low] < xs[j]:
            j = j - 1

        if i >= j then crossed = True
        else swap(xs[i], xs[j])

    swap(xs[low], xs[j])
    return j

{-
   Heapsort, which runs only when the pivots were bad enough to have earned it.

   A sift-down heap over the range, built bottom-up and then drained from the back. Every movement is
   a swap for the reason everything here is, and the whole of it is in place - which is what makes it
   the right fallback rather than a merge sort, whose scratch this would have to allocate at the
   moment a program is already going badly.

   `child` is `low + 2*(parent - low) + 1` said without the multiply, because the range does not
   start at zero and the arithmetic that assumes it does is the one bug this shape reliably has.
-}
fn (Ord(a)) siftDown(&xs: [a], low: Size, root: Size, high: Size) -> {}:
    let &parent = root
    let &settled = False

    while !settled:
        let child = parent + (parent - low) + 1

        if child > high then settled = True
        else:
            let &best = child
            if child < high && xs[child] < xs[child + 1] then best = child + 1

            if xs[parent] < xs[best]:
                swap(xs[parent], xs[best])
                parent = best
            else settled = True

fn (Ord(a)) heapRange(&xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size) -> {}:
    let &start = low + (high - low - 1) / 2
    while start >= low:
        siftDown(xs, low, start, high)
        start = start - 1

    let &end = high
    while end > low:
        swap(xs[low], xs[end])
        end = end - 1
        siftDown(xs, low, low, end)

{-
   The driver: partition, recurse into the smaller side, and loop on the larger.

   Which way round that goes is what bounds the *stack* rather than the running time. The recursive
   call always covers at most half the range, so the depth is at most log₂(n) whatever the pivots do,
   and the side that could be the whole range minus one is the one this loop takes rather than the
   one it calls. Recursing into both would be correct and would put a bad partition's whole depth on
   the stack; the alternative to this loop is the explicit stack `test/bench/programs/Sort.yana` has,
   and an explicit stack is an allocation. That program's own note says a body taking `&xs: [Int]`
   and passing it to itself is rejected, which is why it has one - that is no longer true, and this
   function is the counterexample.

   `budget` is what bounds the running time, and it is separate for that reason: a depth that keeps
   halving is the good case and it exhausts a budget of `2·bitWidth(n)` only when the partitions have
   been lopsided for longer than chance explains. Reaching zero hands the rest to `heapRange`.

   `at - 1` where `at` may be `low` and `low` may be zero: `Size` is signed on both targets, so what
   that produces is -1 and what -1 does is fail `high - low >= smallRange` and then be a range
   `insertionRange` walks zero times. The same is true of the empty container the caller passes as
   `sortRange(window, 0, -1)`. There is no unsigned wrap to guard here and no guard for one.
-}
fn (Ord(a)) sortRange(&xs: [a], first: Size, last: Size, depth: Int) -> {}:
    let &low = first
    let &high = last
    let &budget = depth

    while high - low >= smallRange:
        if budget <= 0:
            heapRange(xs, low, high)
            return

        budget = budget - 1
        let at = partitionRange(xs, low, high)

        if at - low < high - at:
            sortRange(xs, low, at - 1, budget)
            low = at + 1
        else:
            sortRange(xs, at + 1, high, budget)
            high = at - 1

    insertionRange(xs, low, high)

-- The same six, with the comparison supplied at the call. Every line is the `Ord` body above with
-- `xs[p] < xs[q]` written `before(xs[p], xs[q])`; the note at the top of this file is why that is a
-- copy rather than a parameter.
fn insertionRangeBy(&xs: [a], before: (a, a) -> Bool, low: Size, high: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = low + 1

    while i <= high:
        let &j = i
        while j > low && before(xs[j], xs[j - 1]):
            swap(xs[j], xs[j - 1])
            j = j - 1

        i = i + 1

fn partitionRangeBy(&xs: [a], before: (a, a) -> Bool, low: Size, high: Size) -> Size:
    let mid = low + (high - low) / 2
    if before(xs[mid], xs[low]) then swap(xs[mid], xs[low])
    if before(xs[high], xs[low]) then swap(xs[high], xs[low])
    if before(xs[high], xs[mid]) then swap(xs[high], xs[mid])
    swap(xs[mid], xs[low])

    let &i = low
    let &j = high + 1
    let &crossed = False

    while !crossed:
        i = i + 1
        while i < high && before(xs[i], xs[low]):
            i = i + 1

        j = j - 1
        while j > low && before(xs[low], xs[j]):
            j = j - 1

        if i >= j then crossed = True
        else swap(xs[i], xs[j])

    swap(xs[low], xs[j])
    return j

fn siftDownBy(&xs: [a], before: (a, a) -> Bool, low: Size, root: Size, high: Size) -> {}:
    let &parent = root
    let &settled = False

    while !settled:
        let child = parent + (parent - low) + 1

        if child > high then settled = True
        else:
            let &best = child
            if child < high && before(xs[child], xs[child + 1]) then best = child + 1

            if before(xs[parent], xs[best]):
                swap(xs[parent], xs[best])
                parent = best
            else settled = True

fn heapRangeBy(&xs: [a], before: (a, a) -> Bool, low: Size, high: Size) -> {}:
    let &start = low + (high - low - 1) / 2
    while start >= low:
        siftDownBy(xs, before, low, start, high)
        start = start - 1

    let &end = high
    while end > low:
        swap(xs[low], xs[end])
        end = end - 1
        siftDownBy(xs, before, low, low, end)

fn sortRangeBy(&xs: [a], before: (a, a) -> Bool, first: Size, last: Size, depth: Int) -> {}:
    let &low = first
    let &high = last
    let &budget = depth

    while high - low >= smallRange:
        if budget <= 0:
            heapRangeBy(xs, before, low, high)
            return

        budget = budget - 1
        let at = partitionRangeBy(xs, before, low, high)

        if at - low < high - at:
            sortRangeBy(xs, before, low, at - 1, budget)
            low = at + 1
        else:
            sortRangeBy(xs, before, at + 1, high, budget)
            high = at - 1

    insertionRangeBy(xs, before, low, high)

-- The budget the driver starts with: twice the number of bits it takes to write the length, which is
-- 2·⌊log₂ n⌋ + 2 and is introsort's published depth limit plus the two `bitWidth` rounds up by. A
-- perfectly balanced quicksort uses ⌊log₂ n⌋ of it, so what the rest absorbs is ordinary bad luck
-- rather than an adversary.
fn recursionBudget(count: Size) -> Int = 2 * (truncate(bitWidth(count)) :: Int)

{-
   Sorting in place, ascending by `Ord(a)`.

   `elements` and not a `[a]` parameter - the note at the top of this file is why, and it is worth
   reading before changing this signature, because the version that reads better does not compile
   for `sortBy`.
-}
pub fn (Writable(c, a), Ord(a)) sort(&xs: c) -> {}:
    let &window = elementsMut(xs)
    let count = length(window)

    sortRange(window, 0, count - 1, recursionBudget(count))

{-
   The same, with the order supplied at the call.

   `before(left, right)` answers whether `left` comes first, which is `<` and not `compare`: three
   answers would have two of them discarded at every comparison in the loop, and a caller holding an
   `Ordering` writes `compare(l, r) == LT`. Read the pair as "sort with this `<` instead of the
   class's", which is exactly what it is.

   What the comparison has to be is a strict weak order - irreflexive, and consistent about ties.
   One that is not produces a container in some order and nothing worse: the scans in
   `partitionRangeBy` are bounded by the range rather than by the comparison, which is the one thing
   this file spends instructions on to defend.
-}
pub fn (Writable(c, a)) sortBy(&xs: c, before: (a, a) -> Bool) -> {}:
    let &window = elementsMut(xs)
    let count = length(window)

    sortRangeBy(window, before, 0, count - 1, recursionBudget(count))

{-
   A new container, in order, with the argument left as it was.

   Over `Chunked` rather than `Contiguous`, and that is not an oversight: what this reads is
   elements, and a container that keeps them in runs can supply those without promising a single
   buffer address. The sort itself still runs on the `Array(a)` this builds, which is contiguous by
   construction.

   **`Copy(a)`, which `sort` does not ask for.** Copying every element is what "leave the argument
   alone" means, and `Copy` is what says a value can be duplicated at all.

   It was `TrivialCopy(a)` on the reasoning that `Copy` is the authored escape hatch for a type that
   can be duplicated but not bitwise, so asking for it would exclude `Int` rather than include
   `String`. That stopped being true when `Copy` gained its blanket instance over `TrivialCopy`:
   every trivially copyable type is `Copy` now, so this constraint is strictly the weaker of the two
   and `sorted(names)` for an `Array(String)` is what it buys. `Array.yana` states the same
   preference for the same reason at `Append(Array(a))`.

   The element read is `copy(chunk[i])` in `collected` below rather than a bare `chunk[i]`, which is
   what the wider constraint costs and is one call that folds to nothing for a scalar.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Ord(a), Copy(a)) sorted(xs: c) -> Array(a):
    let &out = collected(xs)
    sort(out)
    return out

-- And the same with the order supplied at the call. `sortedBy` needs no `Ord(a)`, which is the one
-- thing it has that `sorted` does not: a container of something with no order at all can still be
-- put in one by a caller that knows what the order is.
pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Copy(a)) sortedBy(xs: c, before: (a, a) -> Bool) -> Array(a):
    let &out = collected(xs)
    sortBy(out, before)
    return out

{-
   The copy both of those start from.

   One `push` per element rather than a block copy, because a chunk's element type is `a` and a
   contiguous run of them is a fact about *this* container rather than about containers. `reserve` is
   not called in front of it for the same reason: `Chunked` promises no total, so the count is not
   known until the walk is over, and asking each chunk for its length twice to find out costs more
   than the doublings do.

   Not `pub`: it is `sorted`'s first half and answers an `Array(a)` in the argument's own order,
   which is a `collect` and belongs beside the iteration protocol rather than in a file about
   ordering. `items` has landed there and this reads through it; what has not is the `collect` this
   would then be a call to.
-}
fn (Chunked(c, a), Copy(a)) collected(xs: c) -> Array(a):
    let &out = [] :: [a]

    for v in items(xs):
        push(out, copy(v))

    return out
